<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guizhou</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guizhou is a mountainous province of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, with Guiyang as its capital. Home to many non-Han peoples such as the Miao, Buyei and Dong, it lay in ancient times within the lands of Yelang and other south-western kingdoms. It became a regular province under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> in 1413 and remains one of China's poorer regions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guizhou.jpg" alt="Guizhou" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Huangdan2060 · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Huangguoshu_Waterfall_in_October_2020,_Picture12.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Guizhou is an inland province of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>–Guizhou Plateau, with an average altitude of about 1,100 metres. Its capital and largest city, Guiyang, lies in the centre. More than 60 per cent of the province is mountains and another 31 per cent hills, leaving very little flat land. A saying holds that sunny weather never lasts more than three days and flat ground never continues for more than three miles. Its rivers lie in the upper reaches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pearl-river">Pearl River</a> basins.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Stone artefacts found at Guanyindong Cave show that humans lived in the area between about 170,000 and 80,000 years ago. The area was first organised as an administrative region of a Chinese empire under the Tang, when it was named Juzhou. In the Mongol-led Yuan dynasty the character ju, meaning a carpenter's square, was changed to gui, meaning precious. The region formally became a province in 1413 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>. In 1832 the population was estimated at five million.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-march">Long March</a> of 1934 and 1935 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> took refuge in the province. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> took Guiyang on 15 November 1949, and the People's Government of Guizhou Province was established on 26 December. Mao Zedong later promoted the relocation of heavy industry to inland provinces such as Guizhou, to protect it from possible foreign attack.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Guizhou is one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s most diverse provinces: minority groups make up more than 37 per cent of the population, among them the Miao, Bouyei, Dong, Tujia and Yi, and the main language is Southwestern Mandarin. The Dong people built the covered bridges known as Wind and Rain Bridges. The province is the home of the liquor Moutai.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Guizhou” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Millions moved instead, to Yunnan, <b>Guizhou</b>, Taiwan and the highlands, and poverty drove some families to sell children under the guise of adoption.</blockquote><a href="/societies/qing-china">Social Hierarchy of Qing China</a></li><li><blockquote>Yelang was a polity of the south-western peoples in western <b>Guizhou</b> and neighbouring Yunnan and Guangxi, emerging in the late Warring States period.</blockquote><a href="/state/yelang">Yelang</a></li><li><blockquote>Wu Sangui, the Ming commander who opened the Shanhai Pass to the Manchus in 1644, conquered south-western China for the Qing Empire, executed the last Southern Ming emperor in 1662 and ruled Yunnan and <b>Guizhou</b> almost independently as a feudatory prince.</blockquote><a href="/state/zhou-wu-sangui">Zhou (Wu Sangui)</a></li></ul></section>
